Grade 1 lateral ligament sprain from inversion injury on uneven ground to ankle
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Focal ATFL tenderness, Mechanism of inversion on uneven ground, Ability to bear weight (Ottawa negative)

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-008
Reason for Visit: Acute ankle pain and swelling post-injury requiring assessment to exclude malleolar or midfoot fracture.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an acute ankle inversion injury causing pain and localized swelling. Urgent evaluation was necessary to apply the Ottawa Ankle Rules, exclude significant bony fracture, and establish safe weight-bearing protocols prior to the next available in-hours consultation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I rolled my ankle on uneven ground and it puffed up immediately, but I can still limp on it.
History of Present Illness: Patient provides a history of rolling the ankle inwards with inversion and plantarflexion on an uneven surface. Symptoms include focal tenderness precisely over the anterior talofibular ligament (ATFL) slightly anterior and inferior to the lateral malleolus. Mild localized anterolateral soft tissue swelling appeared shortly after the injury. The patient retains the ability to bear weight and take four steps independently, both immediately after the injury and currently. Minimal ecchymosis is present.
Functional Status: Ambulating independently but with a deliberate antalgic limp.
Social/Safety Concerns: Navigating uneven outdoor terrain is temporarily unsafe.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 118/72 | HR: 74 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, comfortable at rest, mild distress during active ambulation testing.
Physical Exam: Ankle examination reveals focal ATFL tenderness and mild soft tissue swelling. Complete absence of bony tenderness over the posterior edge or tip of the lateral and medial malleoli, as well as the base of the 5th metatarsal and navicular. Pain is reproduced by passive inversion and plantarflexion of the foot. Anterior drawer test of the ankle is negative, indicating structural competence of the ATFL without laxity. Syndesmosis is intact, evidenced by a negative squeeze test and negative external rotation stress test. Distal neurovascular exam is normal.
Environmental Observations: Patient’s footwear provides inadequate lateral support; hazards minimal indoors.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Grade 1 lateral ligament sprain of the ankle - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Lateral malleolus fracture - Ruled out due to: Absence of bony point tenderness over the distal 6cm of the posterior fibula and ability to bear weight (Ottawa Ankle Rules negative).

Syndesmotic (High ankle) sprain - Ruled out due to: Negative calf squeeze test and negative external rotation stress test.

Fifth metatarsal avulsion fracture - Ruled out due to: Complete absence of point tenderness at the base of the fifth metatarsal.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient sustained an acute inversion ankle injury requiring clinical differentiation between a simple sprain and an occult fracture. On-site assessment utilized the Ottawa Ankle Rules to definitively exclude a malleolar or midfoot fracture, confirming an isolated Grade 1 ATFL sprain. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically necessary to perform this fracture clearance, thereby safely avoiding an unnecessary emergency department presentation for radiography, and to immediately implement compressive and weight-bearing interventions that would be compromised by a delay until morning.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Advised standard RICE management. Prescribed short-term regular NSAIDs for analgesia and anti-inflammatory effect. Plain radiography is not clinically indicated per Ottawa rules.
Positive Behavioral Change: Utilize a compression bandage and elevate the ankle above heart level when resting. This actively reduces hydrostatic pressure in the joint capsule, decreasing swelling and accelerating functional return.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Figure-of-8 ankle compression brace/bandage.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days for functional reassessment or earlier if pain worsens.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department if you become completely unable to bear weight, if the ankle becomes rapidly hot, red, and swollen, or if you develop numbness in your toes.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Ligamentous injuries can take considerable time to heal and require active rehabilitation.
Clinical Course: Swelling and pain will slowly decrease over 1-2 weeks. Ligament maturation and proprioceptive recovery take 4-6 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek care immediately if your foot goes numb, completely changes color, or if you cannot put any weight on it whatsoever tomorrow.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A compression bandage limits fluid pooling in the soft tissues, allowing the ligament to heal without excessive scar tissue stretching.
Actionable Steps: Wear the compression bandage during the day. Elevate your foot above your heart when sitting. Practice balancing on one leg once the pain improves.

SMS INFORMATION

Grade 1 ankle sprain. No x-ray needed. Use compression bandage and elevate. Bear weight as tolerated. GP review in 1 week.

SMS EDUCATION

Wear a compression bandage during the day. Keep foot elevated on a pillow when resting. Walk on it as pain allows.

Grade 1 radiocarpal joint sprain from bracing a fall to wrist
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Diffuse radiocarpal joint pain, FOOSH mechanism, Negative anatomical snuffbox tenderness with normal x-rays

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-009
Reason for Visit: Acute wrist pain following a mechanical fall, requiring assessment to exclude scaphoid or distal radius fracture.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified an acute wrist injury resulting from a fall onto an outstretched hand (FOOSH). Urgent evaluation was necessary to exclude structural fractures, particularly of the scaphoid, and prevent potential avascular necrosis or displacement before daytime imaging could be coordinated.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I tripped and put my hand out to catch myself, and now my wrist aches badly when I move it.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes a fall onto an outstretched hand resulting in forceful dorsiflexion of the wrist. This was followed by mild, diffuse aching pain across the dorsal and volar aspects of the radiocarpal joint. The patient notes mild localized soft tissue swelling but no visible structural deformity or angulation. Pain is exacerbated by extreme ranges of wrist flexion and extension, both active and passive. The patient has preserved the ability to perform a pain-free, strong power grip.
Functional Status: Independent, but utilizing the uninjured hand for complex fine motor tasks.
Social/Safety Concerns: Minor hindrance in executing bilateral manual tasks.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 78 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Alert, holding wrist in a neutral, supported position.
Physical Exam: Upper limb examination reveals diffuse radiocarpal joint pain and mild localized soft tissue swelling. There is complete absence of focal bony point tenderness in the anatomical snuffbox. No point tenderness is elicited over the distal radius or ulnar styloid. Active and passive range of motion are full but provoke pain at extreme limits. Neuromuscular testing shows a normal, strong power grip and negative scaphoid tubercle tenderness with a pain-free axial load of the thumb metacarpal. Normal neurovascular status in the hand with intact sensation, brisk capillary refill, and normal motor function of all fingers.
Environmental Observations: Loose rug identified at the location of the fall; hazard mitigated.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Grade 1 radiocarpal joint sprain - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Scaphoid fracture - Ruled out due to: Complete absence of anatomical snuffbox tenderness, scaphoid tubercle tenderness, and pain on axial loading of the thumb.

Distal radius fracture (Colles) - Ruled out due to: Intact bone contour, absence of severe focal radial point tenderness, and preserved strong grip strength.

Scapholunate dissociation - Ruled out due to: Absence of localized tenderness specifically over the scapholunate interval and no gross carpal instability on movement.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The FOOSH mechanism carries a high probability of occult carpal or distal radial fractures. Meticulous physical examination safely excluded scaphoid involvement and distal radius fractures through negative provocative testing and absence of bony tenderness. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically indicated to rule out structural wrist injuries that would require immediate rigid cast immobilization, allowing safe provision of conservative management and avoiding unnecessary emergency department presentation overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Advised standard supportive care. Standard posterior-anterior and lateral wrist radiographs are not acutely required based on clinical findings but are recommended if pain persists beyond 5 days.
Positive Behavioral Change: Utilize a removable wrist splint for comfort during activities, but deliberately remove it hourly to perform gentle range-of-motion exercises. This prevents severe joint stiffness while providing symptomatic relief.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Removable velcro wrist splint.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 5-7 days if symptoms fail to resolve.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department or local urgent care if the fingers become cold, pale, or numb, or if severe pain suddenly develops.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Occult scaphoid fractures can occasionally present with delayed bony tenderness; dynamic clinical monitoring is required.
Clinical Course: Mild aching typically resolves over 1-2 weeks. Severe loading or push-ups should be avoided for 4 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek further medical care if you develop localized severe pain at the base of your thumb over the next two days, or if your grip strength dramatically weakens.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A wrist splint provides mechanical offloading to the inflamed joint capsule, reducing nociceptive pain signals during movement.
Actionable Steps: Wear the splint for heavy tasks. Remove the splint frequently to draw small circles with your hand. Avoid putting body weight through your hands.

SMS INFORMATION

Grade 1 wrist sprain. Wear a wrist splint for comfort but move hand frequently. Avoid pushing off your hands. GP review in 5 days if pain persists.

SMS EDUCATION

Use a splint to rest the wrist. Take it off every few hours to wiggle your fingers and move your wrist. Do not do push-ups.
